'''''Dryopteris''''' {{IPAc-en|d|r|aɪ|ˈ|ɒ|p|t|ə|r|ᵻ|s}},<ref>''Sunset Western Garden Book,'' 1995:606–607</ref> commonly called the '''wood ferns''', '''male ferns''' (referring in particular to ''[[Dryopteris filix-mas]]''), or '''buckler ferns''', is a [[fern]] genus in the family [[Dryopteridaceae]], subfamily Dryopteridoideae, according to the [[Pteridophyte Phylogeny Group]] classification of 2016 (PPG&nbsp;I).<ref name=PPGI/> There are about 300-400 species in the genus.<ref name=CFLW/><ref name=PPGI/><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Zhang|first1=Li-Bing|last2=Zhang|first2=Liang|last3=Dong|first3=Shi-Yong|last4=Sessa|first4=Emily B|last5=Gao|first5=Xin-Fen|last6=Ebihara|first6=Atsushi|date=2012|title=Molecular circumscription and major evolutionary lineages of the fern genus Dryopteris (Dryopteridaceae)|journal=BMC Evolutionary Biology|volume=12|issue=1|pages=180|doi=10.1186/1471-2148-12-180|issn=1471-2148|pmc=3483261|pmid=22971160 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2012BMCEE..12..180Z }}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite journal|last1=Sessa|first1=Emily B.|last2=Zhang|first2=Li-Bing|last3=Väre|first3=Henry|last4=Juslén|first4=Aino|date=2015-08-01|title=What We Do (and Don't) Know About Ferns: Dryopteris (Dryopteridaceae) as a Case Study|url=http://openurl.ingenta.com/content/xref?genre=article&issn=0363-6445&volume=40&issue=2&spage=387|journal=Systematic Botany|volume=40|issue=2|pages=387–399|doi=10.1600/036364415X688844|bibcode=2015SysBo..40..387S |s2cid=86153986|url-access=subscription}}</ref> The species are distributed in Asia, the Americas,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Hoshizaki |first1=Barbara Joe |last2=Wilson |first2=Kenneth A. |date=1999 |title=The Cultivated Species of the Fern Genus Dryopteris in the United States |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1547452 |journal=American Fern Journal |volume=89 |issue=1 |pages=1–98 |doi=10.2307/1547452 |jstor=1547452 |bibcode=1999AmFJ...89....1H |issn=0002-8444}}</ref> Europe, Africa, and the Pacific islands, with the highest diversity in eastern Asia.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Sessa|first1=Emily B.|last2=Juslén|first2=Aino|last3=Väre|first3=Henry|last4=Chambers|first4=Sally M.|date=March 2017|title=Into Africa: Molecular phylogenetics and historical biogeography of sub-Saharan African woodferns ( Dryopteris )|journal=American Journal of Botany|volume=104|issue=3|pages=477–486|doi=10.3732/ajb.1600392|pmid=28325830|issn=0002-9122|doi-access=free|hdl=10138/182406|hdl-access=free}}</ref> It is placed in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Dryopteridaceae, subfamily Dryopteridoideae, according to the [[Pteridophyte Phylogeny Group]] classification of 2016 (PPG&nbsp;I).<ref name=PPGI/> Many of the species have stout, slowly creeping rootstocks that form a crown, with a vase-like ring of [[frond]]s. The [[sorus|sori]] are round, with a [[peltate]] [[indusium]]. The [[Stipe (botany)|stipe]]s have prominent scales.

[[Hybrid (biology)#Hybrid plants|Hybridization]] and [[polyploidy]] are well-known phenomena in this group, with many species formed via these processes. The [[North American Dryopteris hybrid complex|North American ''Dryopteris'' hybrid complex]] is a well-known example of [[speciation]] via allopolyploid hybridization.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Sessa|first1=Emily B|last2=Zimmer|first2=Elizabeth A|last3=Givnish|first3=Thomas J|date=2012|title=Unraveling reticulate evolution in North American Dryopteris (Dryopteridaceae)|journal=BMC Evolutionary Biology|volume=12|issue=1|pages=104|doi=10.1186/1471-2148-12-104|issn=1471-2148|pmc=3509404|pmid=22748145 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2012BMCEE..12..104S }}</ref>

The fossil record of this genus shows that it was widespread even as far back as the Cretaceous with fossils being found in [[Late Cretaceous]] rock layers from North America<ref>{{Cite journal |last=McIver |first=Elisabeth E |date=2002 |title=The paleoenvironment of Tyrannosaurus rex from southwestern Saskatchewan, Canada |url=https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/10.1139/e01-073 |journal=Canadian Journal of Earth Sciences |volume=39 |issue=2 |pages=207–221 |doi=10.1139/e01-073 |bibcode=2002CaJES..39..207M |issn=0008-4077|url-access=subscription }}</ref> down to Antarctica.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Dutra |first1=Tânia Lindner |last2=Batten |first2=David J. |date=April 2000 |title=Upper Cretaceous floras of King George Island, West Antarctica, and their palaeoenvironmental and phytogeographic implications |url=https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0195667100902210 |journal=Cretaceous Research |language=en |volume=21 |issue=2–3 |pages=181–209 |doi=10.1006/cres.2000.0221|bibcode=2000CrRes..21..181D |url-access=subscription }}</ref> 

==Selected species==
{{main|List of Dryopteris species}}
The genus has a large number of species. The PPG I classification suggested there were about 400 species;<ref name=PPGI/> {{As of|2020|February|lc=yes}}, the ''Checklist of Ferns and Lycophytes of the World'' listed 328 species and 83 hybrids.<ref name=CFLW/> Some genera sunk into ''Dryopteris'', such as ''Dryopsis'' and ''Nothoperanema'', are distinguished by other sources.<ref name=CFLW/>
